{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvAKMED4BD1v2xvdyA1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The four gaps the hook named are now built and tested. The rest of the plan is still unbuilt, and I've listed it below rather than calling it done.\n\n## What changed this round\n\n- **Hot-seat play:** 1–4 humans on one device. In the campaign, each ruler musters in turn behind a pass-the-device screen. In skirmish, a toggle makes both sides human. Battles between two human sides hand control between them.\n- **Shared-world AI Hub mode:** the host copy runs one battle and broadcasts its state after every command. Each other copy sends its own player's commands to the host. A player who stays away for 25 s is played by the host, and a side with no player is run by the planner. The hub round's mode is now `shared-world`, replacing the earlier parallel mode.\n- **Promotions:** each class line has a tier 2 (level 5) and tier 3 (level 9) class, changed for mana with an announcement on the report.\n- **Season Dials and the Clockwork Sanctum:** the final battle at Meridian uses a map with three dials and a gate. A unit ending a move on a dial turns it, and the gate opens when all dials show the battle's season. The dials are drawn on the board.\n- **Statuses and skills:** stun, poison, haste, shield, drain, pierce and sweep, all in the simulator.\n- **Ward and Forecast:** monsters outside three hexes of every friendly knight hit and guard less. The target list shows each strike's hit chance and damage.\n- **Content:** 25 castles, 36 knights, 30 monster species with three forms each, 33 items, 13 battle maps, 45 story events, 15 classes in five lines, and 7 endings (four good ones, one per realm, and three kinds of bad).\n- **Story:** events fire from triggers, such as season, castle captured or lost, candle, and first Dominion march. Choices set flags, spend mana, grant items, or recruit a knight.\n\n## What's still not built\n\n- **Skills:** 21 of the plan's 90.\n- **Unique ruler classes:** none. The plan's eight are not in.\n- **Stillness:** not built.\n- **Bespoke beast rigs:** the plan's custom rig module is not built. Monsters use the engine's Blob and Humanoid rigs.\n- **Music by season:** not built. Music changes by scene only.\n- **Browser playthroughs:** I wrote play tests that drive the real scenes, but not the plan's five full browser playthroughs.\n\nThese are also listed on the About screen under \"Not built\" and in `exit-interview.json`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 531 checks, all passing. Output is one line.\n- AI Hub harness, shared-world mode, all passing: 2 players, 4 players under latency and loss, a host leaving mid-round, and a host with no player.\n- Screenshots of the new screens (Sanctum, story choice, hot-seat pass) show no console errors.\n- Balance: attackers take the Keep about a third of the time. Passive campaigns still end mostly by rival capture, around season seven.\n\n**Not verified:** a real gamepad, and listening to the audio.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:29\n- Source: Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin, 1998)\n- Plan: PLAN.md, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort. Followed in architecture and most content counts. Items listed under \"Not built\" are not implemented.\n- Genre and perspective: turn-based strategy with a tactical hex battle layer. Overhead continent map, tilted hex board for battles, and side-on dialogue scenes.\n- Tech: JavaScript on my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (unchanged), canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client session.js v1.4.0.\n- Art direction: the engine's pixel style. Humanoid and Blob rigs, a painted continent, castles and banners in code, and pixel button glyphs.\n- Animation: engine rigs and E.move strikes, poses and walks, plus a bell-by-bell battle event queue.\n- Audio: five songs, sound effects and Animalese babble, all synthesized in code.\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 2–4 players.\n- Testing: 531 headless checks, balance runs, Playwright screenshots, and the AI Hub harness in four scenarios.\n- Research: the engine's API card, guide, kits and source, and the AI Hub README. No web search.\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown.\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- Usage: unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n- **Issues caught:** eleven across both rounds. Nine in the first round (seven broke play, two were cosmetic). Two in the hub harness this round, where shared-world copies sent too little traffic.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** five in the first round, all fixed. This round had none critical, but the hub round failed twice until copies sent heartbeats and the host re-broadcast its state.\n- **Most helpful test:** the play suite, which drives the real scenes with taps and time, together with the screenshots. The screenshots caught draw errors that headless runs skip.\n- **Least helpful test:** the state-hash check, which only tests the hash function against itself.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** E.Humanoid and E.Blob with the E.move attack states, rigs that pose from plain state every step, and E.Dialog choices, which worked as documented.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** scenes don't receive their game object, so I set it on each scene. Scene changes take a fade, so tests must wait for it. E.Dialog takes one voice sound rather than a function, so I swap the voice each update.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** the battle and campaign simulations, the story triggers, the hex board and its event queue, the list widget, the hub host protocol, and the PlayStation glyphs.\n- **Hardest part:** fitting a tilted hex board to the screen with readable units, and keeping shared-world copies in agreement without a server.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** nothing. The engine is used as shipped.\n- **One thing I'd change:** a per-letter voice callback on E.Dialog, so Animalese can be pitched per character.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n- **What I liked most:** the clear list of what to build and the exact file names, which left no doubt about the deliverables.\n- **What was confusing:** the plan and the brief disagree on file names and scope, and the plan asks for far more content than one session can build well. The title also came from the plan's earlier draw, not one I made.\n- **Hardest thing to do:** keeping the scope honest. The \"Not built\" list is the honest accounting of the gap.\n- **What I'd change:** a single scope line saying which parts of the plan are required and which are optional.\n\n**Files:**\n- `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229.html` (single file, in the work folder root)\n- `exit-interview.json` (beside it, parses, every field filled)\n- Dev folder: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229/`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1159,"cache_read_input_tokens":618790,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1159},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b593beda-ab71-4ff5-909e-67cc1a56f914","uuid":"09a022e5-6300-4798-b792-85d7433b308a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:51:01.672Z","request_id":"req_011CghvAK28Yae714Jz5Voff"}
