{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxMRYpjgWoLXRLfhFSN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The final checks pass on the final build, and the only processes I started have been stopped. Here is where things stand.\n\n## What was added this round\n\n- **Skills:** the plan's full table of 90 skills and spells, with areas, filters, powers, heals, statuses, cooldowns and passives. Old skill ids resolve to the plan's ids.\n- **Classes:** the plan's 23 classes, in five lines of three tiers plus eight unique ones. Knights are assigned to them, and each element picks its own bolt, hex, storm and tempest.\n- **Promotion:** at level 10 and level 20, as the plan says. Few knights reach level 10 in a 12-season campaign, so promotions will be rare until the knight-experience slider is tuned.\n- **Stillness:** from autumn of year three, the Gray Knight stills a castle that borders the Dominion each season. It yields nothing, and its troops can't move, march or quest.\n- **Capture and retreat:** monsters still standing on a losing side can be captured by an adjacent or warded enemy knight, using the plan's chance formula. A commander can retreat, which ends its side's battle.\n- **Ward and forecast:** monsters outside a knight's ward hit and guard less. The target list shows each strike's hit chance and damage.\n- **Beast rigs:** quadruped, drake, avian and serpent bodies, on eight species.\n- **Music:** a theme for each season, attacking and defending battle themes with a tension layer from bell nine, the hourless and Sanctum themes, event and ending themes, and a sting when the season turns.\n- **Rival lines:** two lines between rulers before their battle, once per pair.\n- **Hot-seat, shared-world hub, dials, endings and story events:** carried over and re-tested.\n- **Dev mode:** a gallery of every monster in its three forms. Tutorial tips appear during the first bells of Border Meadow.\n- **Browser playthroughs:** five campaigns through the real screens, to their endings, with no page errors. Run them with `node tests/run.mjs --browser`.\n\n## What's still open\n\n- **Season wipe** between seasons, and **troop figures** on the continent map (banners stand in for them).\n- **Monsters carry one skill each.** The plan's bestiary gives several.\n- **Balance is rough.** Attackers take the Keep about half the time in planner battles, and defenders rarely win by killing the commander. The dev sliders are the place to tune it.\n- **Monster species names** are my own folklore set, not the plan's list.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 538 checks, all passing.\n- Five browser playthroughs: all pass.\n- AI Hub harness, shared-world mode: passes with 2 players, 4 players under latency and loss, a host leaving, and a host with no player.\n\n**Not verified:** a real gamepad, and listening to the audio.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:29\n- Source: Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin, 1998)\n- Plan: PLAN.md, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort. Followed in architecture, mechanics and most content counts. The season wipe, troop figures and balance tuning are still open.\n- Genre and perspective: turn-based strategy with a tactical hex battle layer. Overhead continent map, tilted hex board for battles, and side-on dialogue scenes.\n- Tech: JavaScript on my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (unchanged), canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client session.js v1.4.0.\n- Art direction: the engine's pixel style, with Humanoid and Blob rigs, beast rigs, a painted continent, castles and banners, and pixel button glyphs.\n- Animation: engine rigs and E.move strikes, beast walk and wing motion, and a bell-by-bell battle event queue.\n- Audio: season, battle, hourless, boss, event and ending themes, stings, and Animalese babble, all synthesized in code.\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 2–4 players.\n- Testing: 538 headless checks, five browser playthroughs, balance runs, Playwright screenshots, and the AI Hub harness in four scenarios.\n- Research: the engine's API card, guide and kits, and the AI Hub README. No web search.\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown.\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- Usage: unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n- **Issues caught:** fourteen across all rounds. Nine in the first round, two in the hub harness, and three in the playthroughs and review. One of those three was a campaign that never advanced its season.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** five in the first round, all fixed. This round, the season that never advanced, and a battle that couldn't resume after its rival lines.\n- **Most helpful:** the browser playthroughs, which run whole campaigns through the real screens, together with the play suite and screenshots.\n- **Least helpful:** the state-hash check, which only tests the hash function against itself.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** E.Humanoid and E.Blob with the E.move attack states, rigs that pose from plain state, and E.Dialog choices.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** scenes don't receive their game object; scene changes take a fade, so tests must wait; and E.Dialog takes one voice sound rather than a function.\n- **Built myself:** the battle and campaign simulations, the skill engine, the story triggers, the hex board, the list widget, the hub host protocol, the beast rigs and the glyphs.\n- **Hardest part:** fitting a tilted hex board to the screen, and keeping shared-world copies in agreement without a server.\n- **Engine changes:** none. The engine is used as shipped.\n- **One change I'd make:** a per-letter voice callback on E.Dialog.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** the clear list of what to build and the exact file names.\n- **Confusing:** the plan and the brief disagree on names and scope, and the plan asks for more content than one session can build well. The title came from the plan's earlier draw, not one I made.\n- **Hardest:** keeping the scope honest. Every feature the brief names is built, and most of the plan's mechanics are too. The season wipe, the troop figures and the balance tuning are not finished.\n- **Would change:** a single scope line that separates the plan's required items from its optional ones.\n\n**Files, in the work folder root:**\n- `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- Dev folder: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229/`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1344,"cache_read_input_tokens":784439,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1344},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b593beda-ab71-4ff5-909e-67cc1a56f914","uuid":"1426336a-b13f-4ac6-bac4-c3718d2c52ee","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:19:45.289Z","request_id":"req_011CghxMR6nrH2Zw5pHaSeSe"}
