{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghydvc8rVDye5RHE2uK4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The sound work is done, and the balance work is not. Three of the plan's balance targets still fail on measurement, so I can't tell you the plan is fully met.\n\n## What changed this round\n\n- **Sound effects:** every effect the plan names is now in the game. That covers UI, bell tolls (pitch steps per bell), weapons, spells by season, statuses, outcomes, creatures, world sounds and timers. Battle events, skills, statuses, captures, dial turns, the candle, and the campaign's summons and captures all play them.\n- **Animalese voice:** dialogue voices each character as it's typed. Vowels are tones, consonants are noise ticks, capitals are louder, and punctuation is silent. The Gray Knight speaks in two voices, one seven semitones lower.\n- **Balance work:**\n  - A commander-kill rule, a defender's-guard slider, a player-mana slider, and a 2-knight ruler fix.\n  - The autoplay player now promotes knights and marches only with strong knights.\n  - The plan's Keep rule is in place: the attacker wins only if a knight stands on the Keep at the end of the last bell.\n  - The best settings I found are rival aggression 0.5 and autoplay odds 0.8.\n\n## Balance against the plan's §14.5 targets (measured, 16 games each)\n\n| Target | Measured | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| AI-only campaigns last 12–16 seasons | 9.2 average | not met |\n| No realm wins more than 40% (AI-only) | Verdance 4 of 16 (25%) | met |\n| Player on autoplay reaches Meridian in ≥60% of seeds, seasons 10–14 | 5 of 16 (31%), average 8.9 seasons | not met |\n| Early battles average 8 bells or fewer | 11.7 bells | not met |\n| Hub seats each win 20–30% | not measured | not measured |\n\nThe player's win rate stays around 25–30% across every setting I tried. The likely cause is that the autoplay player has only one knight per march against a garrison, and that's a design limit I didn't change. The sliders are where tuning is meant to happen.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 546 checks, all passing. This includes an audio suite that checks every named effect exists and that the voice is deterministic.\n- `node tests/run.mjs --browser`: five playthroughs, all passing.\n- AI Hub harness, shared-world, 2 players: passing on the final build.\n\n**Not verified:** a real gamepad, and listening to the audio. The audio schedule is tested, but I haven't heard the output.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:29\n- Source: Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin, 1998)\n- Plan: PLAN.md, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort. Its architecture, mechanics, sound-effect list and content counts are followed. Its balance targets are not met on measurement.\n- Genre and perspective: turn-based strategy with a tactical hex battle layer. Overhead continent map, tilted hex board for battles, and side-on dialogue scenes.\n- Tech: JavaScript on my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (unchanged), canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client session.js v1.4.0.\n- Art direction: the engine's pixel style, with Humanoid and Blob rigs, beast rigs, troop figures on the map, and pixel button glyphs.\n- Animation: engine rigs and E.move strikes, beast walk and wing motion, a season wipe, and a bell-by-bell battle event queue.\n- Audio: season, battle, hourless, boss, event and ending themes, stings, the plan's named sound effects, and per-letter Animalese voices, all synthesized in code.\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 2–4 players.\n- Testing: 546 headless checks, five browser playthroughs, balance runs against the plan's targets, Playwright screenshots, and the AI Hub harness in four scenarios.\n- Research: the engine's API card, guide and kits, and the AI Hub README. No web search.\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown.\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- Usage: unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n- **Issues caught:** fourteen across all rounds. Nine in the first round (seven broke play, two cosmetic); two in the hub harness; three in the playthroughs and review. This round, a stray comma broke the build once, and the autoplay runs showed rulers falling in defence.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** five in the first round, all fixed. Later, a campaign that never advanced its season, and a battle that couldn't resume after its rival lines.\n- **Most helpful:** the browser playthroughs, which run whole campaigns through the real screens, together with the balance runs. The balance runs showed the rulers falling in defence, which the playthroughs alone didn't reveal.\n- **Least helpful:** the state-hash check, which only tests the hash function against itself.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** E.Humanoid and E.Blob with the E.move attack states, rigs that pose from plain state, and E.Dialog choices.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** scenes don't receive their game object, so I set it on each scene. Scene changes take a fade, so tests must wait. E.Dialog takes one voice sound rather than a function, so I set the voice before each update and let the engine play it. The engine has no band-pass filter for formants, so the vowel colour is approximated with pitch.\n- **Built myself:** the battle and campaign simulations, the skill engine, the story triggers, the hex board, the list widget, the hub host protocol, the beast rigs, the season wipe, the sound set and the voice.\n- **Hardest part:** fitting a tilted hex board to the screen, and keeping shared-world copies in agreement without a server.\n- **Engine changes:** none. The engine is used as shipped.\n- **One change I'd make:** a per-letter voice callback on E.Dialog, and a band-pass filter on the audio voices.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** the clear list of what to build and the exact file names.\n- **Confusing:** the plan and the brief disagree on names and scope, and the plan's balance targets are numbers that the design doesn't reach with a single-knight march. The title came from the plan's earlier draw, not one I made.\n- **Hardest:** keeping the scope honest. The features are built, and so are the plan's mechanics, sound list and content counts. The balance targets are not met, and I've said so.\n- **Would change:** a single scope line that separates the plan's required items from its targets, and a note on which targets are design goals and which are tests.\n\n**Files, in the work folder root:**\n- `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- Dev folder: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229/`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1248,"cache_read_input_tokens":864809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1248},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b593beda-ab71-4ff5-909e-67cc1a56f914","uuid":"25dadf62-4895-4d7a-a1c8-e796d54ee4f0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:36:34.187Z","request_id":"req_011CghydvDZfdsHQAf3q8B5E"}
