{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghzem3Z2YhtQX9PLfQyM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The balance targets are still not met, and I'm not going to claim they are. The features and artifacts are complete, but the plan's §14.5 targets fail on measurement.\n\n## What changed this round\n\n- **Economy:** moved toward the plan's numbers. Starting mana is 120 for each season-realm and 300 for the Dominion. Capitals yield 40, Meridian 60, other castles 25, with a season bonus of 25%. Summons cost 3× the species cost, and a castle may summon three times a season. Upkeep is paid each season, and Hourless creatures are exempt.\n- **Armies:** several knights can march on one castle as a single battle. Autoplay gathers knights into a staging castle beside its goal, and attacks when the army is strong enough.\n- **Meridian:** the Gray Knight recovers at the start of every season, and the Dominion's knights no longer march out of Meridian.\n- **Tooling:** the simulation tools accept tunables on the command line, and the tests are updated to the new costs.\n\n## Balance against §14.5, measured with the current defaults (12 games each)\n\n| Target | Measured | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| AI-only campaigns last 12–16 seasons | 12.0 average | met, but no realm takes Meridian, so the contest never resolves |\n| No realm wins more than 40% (AI-only) | No realm wins at all | met, trivially |\n| Player reaches Meridian in ≥60% of seeds, seasons 10–14 | 0 of 12 | not met |\n| Early battles average ≤8 bells | 11.7 average | not met |\n| Hub seats win 20–30% each | Not measured | not measured |\n\nThe earlier build, before this economy change, had the player winning about 31% of campaigns. The economy change moved the balance further from the targets for the player. I kept the plan's economy because that's what the plan specifies, but I didn't find a setting that recovers the player's wins.\n\n## Why it's stuck\n\nKnights grow only through battle, and armies are scored by summing knight levels and monster HP. A level-two army can't beat Meridian's garrison, so the player's attacks lose. The fix is in how knights grow and how strength is judged, not in the economy knobs. That's a design change I haven't made.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 546 checks, all passing.\n- `node tests/run.mjs --browser`: five browser playthroughs, all passing. All five end on the Hour-Candle, so none of them exercises the Meridian win path.\n- Not re-run this round: the shared-world hub harness. Hub code and battle code are unchanged since the last passing run, but I haven't rerun it on this exact build.\n\n**Not verified:** a real gamepad, and listening to the audio.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:29\n- Source: Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin, 1998)\n- Plan: PLAN.md, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort. Its architecture, mechanics, sound-effect list and content counts are followed. Its balance targets (§14.5) are not met.\n- Genre and perspective: turn-based strategy with a tactical hex battle layer. Overhead continent map, tilted hex board for battles, and side-on dialogue scenes.\n- Tech: JavaScript on my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (unchanged), canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client session.js v1.4.0.\n- Art direction: the engine's pixel style, with Humanoid and Blob rigs, beast rigs, troop figures on the map, and pixel button glyphs.\n- Animation: engine rigs and E.move strikes, beast walk and wing motion, a season wipe, and a bell-by-bell battle event queue.\n- Audio: season, battle, hourless, boss, event and ending themes, stings, the plan's named sound effects, and per-letter Animalese voices, all synthesized in code.\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 2–4 players.\n- Testing: 546 headless checks, five browser playthroughs, balance runs against the plan's targets, Playwright screenshots, and the AI Hub harness.\n- Research: the engine's API card, guide and kits, and the AI Hub README. No web search.\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown.\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- Usage: unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n- **Issues caught:** fourteen across all rounds. Nine in the first round (seven broke play, two cosmetic); two in the hub harness; three in the playthroughs and review.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** five in the first round, all fixed. Later, a campaign that never advanced its season, and a battle that couldn't resume after its rival lines.\n- **Most helpful:** the balance runs, which showed the economy and garrison problems that the unit tests couldn't see, together with the browser playthroughs.\n- **Least helpful:** the state-hash check, which only tests the hash function against itself.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** E.Humanoid and E.Blob with the E.move attack states, rigs that pose from plain state, and E.Dialog choices.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** scenes don't receive their game object, so I set it on each scene. Scene changes take a fade, so tests must wait. E.Dialog takes one voice sound rather than a function, so I set the voice before each update. The engine has no band-pass filter, so vowel colour is approximated with pitch.\n- **Built myself:** the battle and campaign simulations, the skill engine, the story triggers, the hex board, the list widget, the hub host protocol, the beast rigs, the season wipe, the sound set and the voice.\n- **Hardest part:** fitting a tilted hex board to the screen, and getting balance right on a campaign whose strength is scored by summed levels.\n- **Engine changes:** none. The engine is used as shipped.\n- **One change I'd make:** a per-letter voice callback on E.Dialog, and a band-pass filter on the audio voices.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n- **Liked most:** the clear list of what to build and the exact file names.\n- **Confusing:** the plan and the brief disagree on names and scope, and the plan's balance targets are numbers this design doesn't reach without changing how knights grow. The title came from the plan's earlier draw, not one I made.\n- **Hardest:** keeping the scope honest. The features are built, and so are the plan's mechanics, sound list and content counts. The balance targets are not met, and I've said so.\n- **Would change:** a single scope line that separates the plan's required items from its design targets, and a note on which targets are tests and which are design goals.\n\n**Files, in the work folder root:**\n- `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- Dev folder: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050229/`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1484,"cache_read_input_tokens":932637,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1484},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b593beda-ab71-4ff5-909e-67cc1a56f914","uuid":"25ed2c5b-2ae5-424a-b407-0a3ae7590d6f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:49:52.886Z","request_id":"req_011Cghzekan9YFzd6kAfWV4z"}
